A||||10||William||swapped in 120 gauges..Tach reads low?||||1||
Z||000000||William||12-26-2000||07:43 PM||||Over the holidays I swapped in the 120 mph gauge set that I got for christmas. The gauges sure look nice at night, but tonight I got the feeling that the tach is reading to low at higher rpm's. At idle it actually reads about 300 rpm to high. I'm not sure if I'm just imagining this or it is really happening, but it sounds like the car is near redline at 4500. My car is a 85 SE with a V-6 and I asumme all backlit tachs, (with no oil gauge),are from v-6 cars. How can I test the tach? I sure don't want to swap the old pod back in to test the rpm's at various speeds, but I don't know what else to do. I don't trust the tach now and I'm afraid I might sling a rod if I did. This could all be my imagination of course.||||reg||1
Z||000001||80's BOY||12-26-2000||08:02 PM||||how odd....i just did the same swap and my tach is way, way too high. 85 gt with 87 backlit gages now.<P>could it be the "tach filter"? what is this mysterious tach filter, and what effect does it have on the engine's performance and how??<p>[This message has been edited by 80's BOY (edited 12-26-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000002||terryk||12-26-2000||08:32 PM||||Other than being the wrong tach, nothing else can make it wrong (other than the normal error they have and 300 RPM at hi rpm's isn't unusual).<P>You can dial the tach in by finding the 2554339 chip, cutting the traces to pin 5 and 8 and soldering a 50k pot (a little trimmer pot is fine) across them. You will need a scanner or remote tach, wind it up to 3-4k and turn the pot to make the tach reading match.||||reg||1
Z||000003||80's BOY||12-26-2000||08:47 PM||||dude, you're smart. i guess i just got learned.||||reg||7
